(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-abnormal-cell-growth-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Abnormal Cell Growth and Differentiation Quiz 2 — Clin Path I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which statement correctly defines cancer and gives the origin of its name?","choices":["A malignant neoplasm; from the Latin for crab","Any neoplasm at all; from the Latin for growth, because the mass enlarges without a stimulus","A neoplasm that has metastasized; from the Greek for wandering, because cells migrate","A precancerous lesion; from the Latin for disorder, because architecture is lost"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — so named because it adheres to any tissue it seizes upon and reaches out with claws, invading surrounding tissue. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which inherited tumor suppressor alteration is associated with malignant melanoma?","choices":["Adenomatous polyposis coli","BRCA-1","p16, also called INK4a","Retinoblastoma protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — p16 (INK4a) is an inherited tumor suppressor alteration associated with malignant melanoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What does the degree of differentiation of a neoplasm measure?","choices":["How many of its cells are in the proliferative pool at any moment","How closely it resembles comparable normal cells in appearance and function","How far it has spread from the primary site within the patient","How rapidly the tumor mass is enlarging over time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — differentiation is the degree a neoplasm resembles comparable normal cells, which is the basis of histological grading. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How differentiated are malignant tumors?","choices":["Always poorly differentiated","Always anaplastic","Always well differentiated","Various degrees of differentiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — malignancy does not mean uniformly poor differentiation. Benign tumors are usually well differentiated. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes Epstein Barr virus in carcinogenesis?","choices":["Gastric adenocarcinoma and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma; it colonizes the stomach","B cell lymphomas and nasopharyngeal carcinoma; it infects B lymphocytes","Hepatocellular carcinoma; it infects hepatocytes and causes chronic injury with regeneration","Cervical carcinoma; it infects cervical epithelium and integrates its genome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it immortalizes them, which is the key step, and it also infects epithelial cells of the oropharynx. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which tumors are associated with an inherited alteration of the retinoblastoma protein?","choices":["Neurofibromatosis types 1 and 2, with tumors of the central and peripheral nervous system","Malignant melanoma","Familial adenomatous polyposis and colon cancer","Retinoblastoma, a rare childhood tumor of the eye, and osteosarcoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same tumor suppressor, two very different tumors. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which four features define dysplasia?","choices":["Disordered epithelial growth, varied cell size and shape, lost architecture, darker larger nuclei","Orderly growth, typically mesenchymal; uniform cell size; preserved architecture; nuclei of normal size and staining","Complete replacement of one mature epithelium by another mature epithelium, with normal architecture retained","Invasion through the basement membrane with spread to regional lymph nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — dysplasia is disordered epithelial growth with varied cell size and shape, lost architectural orientation and darker, larger nuclei; it may progress to cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does falling differentiation mean for the behavior of a tumor?","choices":["It becomes more aggressive","It becomes less aggressive, because poorly differentiated cells divide more slowly","Aggressiveness is unrelated to differentiation and depends only on stage","It becomes more aggressive only once the basement membrane has been breached"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — poorly differentiated cells stay in the proliferative pool, so the tumor grows faster; benign tumors are usually well differentiated and slow growing. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Besides cervical cancer, which malignancies is high-risk human papillomavirus associated with?","choices":["Retinoblastoma and osteosarcoma","Anal, vulvar, vaginal, penile and oropharyngeal cancer","Hepatocellular carcinoma and gastric adenocarcinoma","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma and certain B cell lymphomas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — high-risk human papillomavirus is associated with all five in addition to cervical cancer, the oropharyngeal one being a squamous cell carcinoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes the inherited defect in xeroderma pigmentosum?","choices":["Defective apoptosis genes, so damaged cells cannot self destruct; increased risk of lymphoma","Defective protooncogenes, so growth signaling is lost; increased risk of aplasia","Defective repair genes, so ultraviolet B damage cannot be repaired","Defective tumor suppressor genes, so cell growth cannot be inhibited; increased risk of nervous system tumors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the consequence is an increased risk of skin cancer in sun-exposed areas. It is a repair-gene disease rather than a tumor suppressor one. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What is metaplasia?","choices":["Enlargement of an organ because its individual cells enlarge under increased demand","Autonomous growth of an abnormal mass of tissue derived from a single genetically altered cell","Change of one cell type to another under chronic irritation or injury","Disordered growth of epithelial cells with variation in size and shape and loss of architectural orientation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it involves a change in differentiation. Bladder transitional epithelium becoming squamous is the standard example. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why does skeletal muscle hypertrophy rather than divide?","choices":["Striated muscle cells do not divide, so each fiber gets larger instead","Resistance training recruits satellite cells, so the number of muscle fibers increases","Protein intake causes fluid retention within the muscle, so the organ appears larger","Resistance training converts slow fibers to fast fibers, changing the tissue type"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — skeletal muscle is a permanent tissue, so it grows by enlarging each existing fiber, which is hypertrophy rather than hyperplasia. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is a malignant tumor of mesenchymal origin called?","choices":["A papilloma","A sarcoma","A carcinoma","An adenoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — mesenchymal means supportive tissue, and the malignant form is the sarcoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What happens when a protooncogene mutates?","choices":["It becomes a tumor suppressor gene, which then inhibits cell growth excessively","It becomes a repair gene, which then introduces errors into the genome","It becomes silent, so the cell loses its ability to grow at all","It becomes an oncogene, which encodes oncoproteins that promote continued uncontrolled growth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — mutation converts a protooncogene to an oncogene, whose oncoproteins promote continued growth in an uncontrolled manner. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Where does the gene damage in carcinogenesis come from?","choices":["It arises only from errors during deoxyribonucleic acid replication","Inherited, and/or acquired from chemicals, radiation or microbes","It is always inherited through the germline and never acquired during life","It is always acquired during life and never inherited"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — damaged genes may be inherited or develop from chemical carcinogens, ultraviolet and ionizing radiation, and microbes: viruses and one bacterium. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What are the steps of hematogenous spread, in order?","choices":["Cells separate and degrade intercellular tissue with enzymes, invade the vessel, then travel","Tumor cells adhere more tightly to each other; the mass compresses a vessel; a single fragment travels to one other organ","Tumor cells enter lymphatic vessels at the margin and follow the natural route of drainage","Tumor invades through an organ surface into a body cavity and settles on the serosal membranes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — multiple tumor fragments travel to other organs, so one organ may end up with several metastatic nodules. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"On the dysplasia spectrum, what separates severe dysplasia from invasive cancer?","choices":["The basement membrane","The degree of nuclear enlargement, which is greater in invasive cancer","The presence of mitoses, which appear only once invasion has occurred","Involvement of the regional lymph nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — carcinoma in situ has not invaded through the basement membrane, while invasive cancer has broken through it. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which chemical carcinogens are classically emphasized in occupational bladder cancer?","choices":["Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons","Alkylating agents","Asbestos fibers","Aromatic amines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — aromatic amines are classically emphasized in occupational bladder cancer, whereas tobacco supplies polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes Helicobacter pylori?","choices":["A virus that integrates its genome into gastric epithelial cells","A fungus that colonizes the gastric mucosa in immunosuppressed patients","A Gram-negative bacterium causing chronic gastritis","A Gram-positive bacterium that colonizes the stomach, causing acute ulceration and perforation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it colonizes the stomach and may lead to atrophic gastritis and intestinal metaplasia. It is the one bacterium among the microbial causes of cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does anaplasia mean?","choices":["Close resemblance to the comparable normal cell","Failure of a primordial tissue to develop into a mature organ","Disordered growth of epithelium with retained architecture","Lack of differentiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the extreme of the grading scale, with no resemblance to the normal cell. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What do promoters do?","choices":["They cause the permanent deoxyribonucleic acid damage that starts the process","They repair the deoxyribonucleic acid damage caused by initiators","They cause tumor cells to detach and enter the circulation","They drive proliferation of cells already damaged by an initiator"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sustained or enhanced proliferation raises the risk of successive mutations leading to cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What are mesenchymal tumors derived from?","choices":["Neural tissue: peripheral nerves, nerve sheaths and the central nervous system","Supportive tissue: connective tissue, adipose tissue, cartilage, smooth and striated muscle, and bone","Epithelial surfaces: skin, gastrointestinal lining, respiratory lining and glandular ducts","Hematopoietic tissue: bone marrow, lymph nodes, spleen and thymus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same origin gives sarcomas when the tumor is malignant. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What do initiators do in chemical carcinogenesis?","choices":["They repair damage already caused by another carcinogen","They trigger apoptosis in cells with damaged genomes","They cause permanent damage to deoxyribonucleic acid","They cause sustained or enhanced proliferation of already-damaged cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — permanence is what distinguishes initiation from promotion. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Is metastatic spread random?","choices":["No — it is determined by blood flow, receptors, and genetically determined fitness","Yes — circulating cells lodge in whichever capillary bed they reach first","Yes for hematogenous spread, no for lymphatic spread","No — but only because lymphatic drainage is anatomically fixed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — metastatic spread is not random but set by venous blood flow, tumor and endothelial receptors, and genetic fitness. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What are the four grades of neoplastic differentiation, from most to least resemblance?","choices":["Well differentiated, moderately differentiated, poorly differentiated, anaplasia","Anaplasia, poorly differentiated, moderately differentiated, well differentiated","In situ, microinvasive, invasive, metastatic","Stage I, stage II, stage III, stage IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — differentiation runs from close resemblance (well) through intermediate and poor resemblance to anaplasia, the lack of differentiation. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which three processes link chronic hepatitis B infection to hepatocellular carcinoma?","choices":["Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, and abnormal differentiation","Self-renewal, immortality, and differentiation capacity","Chronic inflammation, regenerative hyperplasia, genomic instability","Initiation, promotion, and progression"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — chronic infection causes ongoing liver cell injury, stimulating continuous regenerative proliferation of liver cells that are at risk for genetic mutations. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is a carcinoma showing squamous cell differentiation called?","choices":["A papilloma","A sarcoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","An adenocarcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a carcinoma with squamous cell differentiation is a squamous cell carcinoma; one with glandular growth is an adenocarcinoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why are genes promoting self destruction of cells with damaged deoxyribonucleic acid necessary?","choices":["To limit the total number of cells an organ can contain","To supply nutrients to neighbouring cells as they proliferate","To slow the rate at which stem cells commit to differentiation","To stop the damage becoming permanent in dividing cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — apoptosis prevents a mutation being carried on into the daughter cells. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What do Nx, N0 and N1 to N2 or higher each mean?","choices":["Nx, nodes removed surgically; N0, nodes present but normal in size; N1 upwards, enlarged nodes","Cannot be assessed; none; increasing number and range involved","Nx, no regional nodal metastasis; N0, nodes cannot be assessed; N1 upwards, distant metastasis","Nx, nodes not yet sampled; N0, one node involved; N1 upwards, two or more nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — x always means cannot be assessed, 0 always means none found, and the numbers rise with the number and range of nodes involved. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How does aplasia differ from agenesis?","choices":["The organ enlarges because its individual cells enlarge","The primordial tissue exists but fails to develop into the mature organ","The primordial tissue never forms, so the organ is completely absent","The organ develops only partially, leaving a functional deficiency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — in aplasia the primordial tissue exists but fails to develop into the mature organ, whereas in agenesis it never formed at all. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"}]};
